+    "Triage (s4, 2026-10-23 07:10Z, RemoteTrigger payload echo, accepted, narrowed), verbatim:\nA stub that pins the pushed commit, not the ref name.\n\nMeasured facts: tools/audit/seat/cloud_dispatch.py already publishes dispatch/<role>-<topic> (PAYLOAD.json and PROMPT.md), but render() (:171-:205) still builds the event message from _prompt(..., brief), with the brief inlined (:167-168, \"snapshotted when this payload was rendered\"). Each trigger update resends the whole ccr, so the 11 KB brief is echoed in every update and run; the proposer's measure (~35k tokens per fire, ~140k for 4) is the orchestrator's observation, not re-measured from git.\nConstraint 1: the inlined brief is deliberate (\"a brief edited afterwards reaches no running seat\"), so a stub that names only brief/<topic> would let a later push change a running seat's brief. The stub must name the dispatch commit SHA (git fetch origin dispatch/<role>-<topic>, then read PROMPT.md at that SHA), which keeps the snapshot; never a moving ref.\nConstraint 2: the stub keeps the first-line grammar, role, head, nonce, pr, topic and FOOTER_BAN inline (a reviewer's verdict is matched to its dispatch by nonce); only the brief body moves.\nConstraint 3: the host-path refusal (HOME regex) must still run on the published PROMPT.md, since it is what the seat reads.\nConstraint 4: the seat needs git read access to origin; the ccr already lists the repository as a source and allows Bash, but one fire on the stub path must prove the seat reads the brief before the mode becomes the default.\nOrder: after DESIGN-STAGES, or put the stub in the same render change, so two PRs do not both edit render(). R10-FLOW-DISPATCH owns this file but is done, so it is not the destination.\nCost: S-M, about 40-70 lines in cloud_dispatch.py (a --stub flag or default, SHA from the dispatch push) plus self-test rows: stub payload under 1 KB with the brief absent, the SHA present, a host path in the brief still refused, and a null contr